mpressed with my work. So, in the end, I got accepted.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong>Tips</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>Well, the key to getting accepted in GSOC is the communication with mentors. Ask questions and get to know what mentors actually want from that idea.</h3>\r\n<h3>And also, keep reading and learning stuff while coding. My knowledge of Linux and programming was really a huge help here, that I was able to understand stuff in such few days.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">UPDATE</span>:-</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>Here my GSoC project -</h3>\r\n<a href=\"https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/projects/#5062300487122944\">https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/projects/#5062300487122944</a>\r\n<h3>Here my GSoC final report -</h3>\r\n<a href=\"https://gist.github.com/sanjitchak/3d0a3eae122aa8f1c8ef35612de89d87\">https://gist.github.com/sanjitchak/3d0a3eae122aa8f1c8ef35612de89d87</a>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;', 'GSOC Journey', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '107-revision-v1', '', '', '2018-08-10 16:12:53', '2018-08-10 10:42:53', '', 107, 'http://techwinky.com/107-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(116, 1, '2018-08-23 08:49:51', '2018-08-23 03:19:51', '<h1><strong>How did I get to know?</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>Well, around December 2016, I was chatting with some stranger in one of facebook messenger bot - Chatible. He was a former student of IIT Bombay and was working in a good company. I asked him about tips for growing myself, where he introduced me to Google Summer of Code.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong>Fast forward to 2017 February</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>In the middle of February, I was searching for organizations. Yeah, it was too late but I just wanted to try. Since I knew C programming quite well at that time, I looked into an organization with only C. It was Libreswan. I searched the whole Internet, reading about IPSEC and networking stuff and trying to choose an idea.</h3>\r\n<h3>In the end, I was not even able to submit a proposal. So, I decided to upgrade my skills first before attempting GSOC again. During that year, I learned Linux properly, worked on some projects and did few courses on some programming concepts (including compiler, assembly language).</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong>GSOC 2018 </strong></h1>\r\n<h3>I was not planning on taking GSOC this year as I thought of upgrading my skills first. I learned Android in the month of January and did few projects on it. I joined one internship in android development and I worked on few apps.</h3>\r\n<h3>At the end of January, I became quite good in Android Development. So, I thought of searching for some organizations for Android and try applying. There, I found one organization - AMAHI, which wanted to improve their Android app that connects to the HOME SERVER. So, I started reading their Android Codebase. I raised PRs, commented on many stuff and talked to mentors in their Android Github repo comment section. I worked on their Android app navigation drawer and raised a pull request.</h3>\r\n<h3>Now, you might think, I might be working on their Android app, right? Well, actually I wanted to but I guess, my fate had other plans. On 21 February, our mentor organized a hangout for interested GSOC candidates to discuss stuff. There I got chance to ask questions directly to mentor on Video Call.</h3>\r\n<h3>While asking questions, I asked some question about one of their other project on INSTALLER because it involves Linux scripts and I was quite experienced in it. And it turned out, I strike the right chord, or whatever you may say. The mentor was very much interested in that project but there were few takers for that project. So, I asked more questions about it. He then told me that if I can execute one module in anaconda, he may get impressed.</h3>\r\n<h3>And so, for the next few days, I spent around 12 hours/day researching about the project and ways to accomplish it. My mentor also emailed me with suggestions and what he expects, which really helped me a lot.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong>The Proposal (<a href=\"https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/projects/#5062300487122944\">here</a>)</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>The project was on customizing anaconda installer, which is an official Installer of Redhat and Fedora Linux. Amahi is a home server,  which gets installed on top of Fedora. And I have to add a GUI spoke during Fedora Installation and automate the installation process. Right now, to set up the Amahi Server, the user has to type commands in the command line, which is not so user-friendly.</h3>\r\n<h3>I searched and read everything about anaconda and Linux stuff and made a proposal in 3-4 days while getting the suggestion from my mentor. I basically spent my whole day writing a proposal and trying to add a GUI spoke in the installation. In the end, I was at least half successful in adding a GUI spoke, so, I confidently submitted my proposal.</h3>\r\n<h3></h3>\r\n<h1><strong>Review Period and Acceptance</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>During the review period, I chatted with my mentor a lot and added a GUI spoke in the installation properly without any errors. I even managed to push one PR in <a href=\"https://github.com/rhinstaller/hello-world-anaconda-addon/pull/12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rhinstaller</a> also.</h3>\r\n<h3>He was quite impressed with my work. So, in the end, I got accepted.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong>Tips</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>Well, the key to getting accepted in GSOC is the communication with mentors. Ask questions and get to know what mentors actually want from that idea.</h3>\r\n<h3>And also, keep reading and learning stuff while coding. My knowledge of Linux and programming was really a huge help here, that I was able to understand stuff in such few days.</h3>\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<h1><strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">UPDATE</span>:-</strong></h1>\r\n<h3>Here my GSoC project -</h3>\r\n<a href=\"https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/projects/#5062300487122944\">https://summerofcode.withgoogle.com/projects/#5062300487122944</a>\r\n<h3>Here my GSoC final report -</h3>\r\n<a href=\"https://gist.github.com/sanjitchak/3d0a3eae122aa8f1c8ef35612de89d87\">https://gist.github.com/sanjitchak/3d0a3eae122aa8f1c8ef35612de89d87</a>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;', 'GSOC Journey', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '107-revision-v1', '', '', '2018-08-23 08:49:51', '2018-08-23 03:19:51', '', 107, 'http://techwinky.com/107-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(120, 1, '2019-03-01 14:24:25', '2019-03-01 08:54:25', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">While creating a <g class=\"gr_ gr_53 gr-alert gr_gramm gr_inline_cards gr_run_anim Style multiReplace\" id=\"53\" data-gr-id=\"53\">website ,</g> choosing colors is important. But sometimes it\'s hard to decide how many colors to use while building a website. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">So, fear not, it\'s quite simple. As a rule of thumb, using maximum 2-3 colors in your whole website is more than enough. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Not convinced?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Check these big companies website:-</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol><li><a href=\"https://www.apple.com\">https://www.apple.com</a> (uses the shades of black and white)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.amazon.in\">https://www.amazon.in</a> (uses a combination of dark blue, orange and white)</li><li><a href=\"http://facebook.com\">http://facebook.com </a>(uses a combination of blue and white)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page</a> (uses a combination of black and white)</li><li><a href=\"http://yahoo.com\">http://yahoo.com</a> (shades of Violet, and white)  </li></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">And I can list many such great websites, having 2-3 colors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Now, you might ask, how is this even possible? Well, it\'s simple psychology. If the visitor of the website is bombarded with many colors, then he/she might get confused. So, using 2-3 colors simplifies things for the visitor. You may use varieties of shades of that same color. But as usual, don\'t overdo it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\"><u>Conclusion</u>:-</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Use at most 2-3 colors for your website. It simplifies stuff for the visitor and gives a clean look to your great website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Quantity of Colors to use in a website', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'quantity-of-colors-to-use-in-a-website', '', '', '2019-03-01 14:26:49', '2019-03-01 08:56:49', '', 0, 'http://techwinky.com/?p=120', 0, 'post', '', 0),
