fused. So, using 2-3 colors simplifies things for the visitor. You may use varieties of shades of that same color. But as usual, don\'t overdo it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\"><u>Conclusion</u>:-</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Use at most 2-3 colors for your website. It simplifies stuff for the visitor and gives a clean look to your great website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'Quantity of Colors to use in a website', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '120-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-03-01 14:23:34', '2019-03-01 08:53:34', '', 120, 'http://techwinky.com/120-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(126, 1, '2019-03-04 16:42:44', '2019-03-04 11:12:44', '<h1><img class=\"wp-image-26 alignright\" src=\"http://techwinky.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/10/IMG_20170219_110354-e1508489263874.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"183\" height=\"184\" />My Story:-</h1>\n<h5>Hi! I am Sanjit Chakrabarti. I am doing Btech in computer engineering, graduating in 2019.</h5>\n<h5>After learning to code in my second semester, I started using only Linux in 2016. I started with Ubuntu first, as usual. I then finished reading this Book \'<a href=\"http://linuxcommand.org/tlcl.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Linux Command Line</a>\' and did a course on Linux administration in Udemy (LINK:- <a href=\"https://www.udemy.com/linux-administration/learn/v4/\">https://www.udemy.com/linux-administration/learn/v4/</a> ).</h5>\n<h5>Then after tinkering with Ubuntu for months, I installed Arch Linux on my PC to get more control over my OS. I am now a happy user of Arch Linux.</h5>\n<h5>In 2018, I did GSoC and successfully completed it too. Now, my main aim is to become a Web developer, specializing in React.</h5>\n<h5>So, I opened this blog to share some great tips and tutorials related to web development.</h5>\n<h5>In free time, I like to cycle far away places and love to volunteer in an NGO.</h5>\n<h5></h5>\n<h5>Follow me on:-</h5>\n<h5>Twitter - <a href=\"https://twitter.com/sujitsanjit?lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">@sujitsanjit</a></h5>\n<h5>Linkedin - <a href=\"http://www.linkedin.com/in/sanjit-chakrabarti-pro\">sanjit-chakrabarti-pro</a></h5>\n<h5>Check out my work -  <a href=\"https://github.com/sanjitchak\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">sanjitchak</a></h5>', 'About Me', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '24-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-03-04 16:42:44', '2019-03-04 11:12:44', '', 24, 'http://techwinky.com/24-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(127, 1, '2019-03-04 17:01:26', '2019-03-04 11:31:26', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Let\'s start with a real-life example. When you cook a packet of rice, you cook whole rice at once. Right? And how do you cook it? By putting in some kind of container (Here, rice cooker) Right? </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Similarly, to cook a particular part of CSS, we enclose that part in a container. For eg, we put the navigation bar in one container and then footer in another container. Or better say, we change properties of all the elements in that container, at once. This saves a lot of time as we don\'t have to change properties of all the element one by one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:code -->\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code\"><code>&lt;div class=\"container\">\n            &lt;img src=\"assets/nav.jpeg\" class=\"logo\" alt=\"navimage\" />\n            &lt;ul>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">PRODUCTS&lt;/a>\n                    &lt;ul>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">LED&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">FAN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">ACCESSORIES&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">WIRES AND CABLES&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">Electronics&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                    &lt;/ul>\n                &lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>\n                    &lt;a href=\"#\">ABOUT US&lt;/a>\n                    &lt;ul>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\"> QUERY &lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">PHONE&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                    &lt;/ul>\n                &lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">CUSTOMER LOGIN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">ADMIN LOGIN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">Search&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n            &lt;/ul>\n        &lt;/div></code></pre>\n<!-- /wp:code -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">In the above, the navigation bar is in a container and so, properties of all the elements (li, ul, a) in that container can be set, all at once.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:code -->\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code\"><code>.container {\nwidth: 80%;\nmargin: 0 auto;\n}</code></pre>\n<!-- /wp:code -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">In the above styling, we set all elements\' width to take 80% of the container.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\"><strong><u>Conclusion</u></strong>:-</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">The container is a great way to set properties of all the elements, at once.<br>This includes -margin, padding, alignment, font &amp; color.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'What is container class? Why use it?', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'what-is-container-class-why-use-it', '', '', '2019-03-04 17:01:34', '2019-03-04 11:31:34', '', 0, 'http://techwinky.com/?p=127', 0, 'post', '', 0),
(128, 1, '2019-03-04 17:01:26', '2019-03-04 11:31:26', '<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Let\'s start with a real-life example. When you cook a packet of rice, you cook whole rice at once. Right? And how do you cook it? By putting in some kind of container (Here, rice cooker) Right? </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">Similarly, to cook a particular part of CSS, we enclose that part in a container. For eg, we put the navigation bar in one container and then footer in another container. Or better say, we change properties of all the elements in that container, at once. This saves a lot of time as we don\'t have to change properties of all the element one by one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:code -->\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code\"><code>&lt;div class=\"container\">\n            &lt;img src=\"assets/nav.jpeg\" class=\"logo\" alt=\"navimage\" />\n            &lt;ul>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">PRODUCTS&lt;/a>\n                    &lt;ul>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">LED&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">FAN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">ACCESSORIES&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">WIRES AND CABLES&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">Electronics&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                    &lt;/ul>\n                &lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>\n                    &lt;a href=\"#\">ABOUT US&lt;/a>\n                    &lt;ul>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\"> QUERY &lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                        &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">PHONE&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                    &lt;/ul>\n                &lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">CUSTOMER LOGIN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">ADMIN LOGIN&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n                &lt;li>&lt;a href=\"#\">Search&lt;/a>&lt;/li>\n            &lt;/ul>\n        &lt;/div></code></pre>\n<!-- /wp:code -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">In the above, the navigation bar is in a container and so, properties of all the elements (li, ul, a) in that container can be set, all at once.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:code -->\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code\"><code>.container {\nwidth: 80%;\nmargin: 0 auto;\n}</code></pre>\n<!-- /wp:code -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">In the above styling, we set all elements\' width to take 80% of the container.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\"><strong><u>Conclusion</u></strong>:-</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"medium\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size\">The container is a great way to set properties of all the elements, at once.<br>This includes -margin, padding, alignment, font &amp; color.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->', 'What is container class? Why use it?', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '127-revision-v1', '', '', '2019-03-04 17:01:26', '2019-03-04 11:31:26', '', 127, 'http://techwinky.com/127-revision-v1/', 0, 'revision', '', 0);
